  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CO- Water and Safety Project</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam is proposing a 12-month integrated WASH and Protection intervention targeting 65,820 individuals (33,424 female, and 32,396
male) in North Gaza and Gaza Governorates. The displaced populations and returnees of the Gaza strip currently face severe
challengesin Water, Sanitation, and Hygiene (WASH) services, exacerbated by destroyed or damaged infrastructure and fuel
shortages. Oxfam&#x2019;s community consultations in March 2025 revealed catastrophic levels of humanitarian needs in Gaza and North
GazaGovernorates, with 80%of WaSH infrastructure destroyed and water production critically impacted, approximately 65% of
population have access to less than6 liters per person per day suitable for drinking and cocking, and at risk of complete shutdown.
Water quality is also a major concern, as 79% of samples fail to meet SPHERE standards, posing serious public health risks. The
sanitation crisis is further exacerbated by waste accumulation, with one million people reporting threats from rodents and pests (76%),
solid waste (54%), sewage (46%), and human waste (34%). Displacement remains widespread, with 100% of respondents in the
2024 protection assessments reporting displacement and 81% having been displaced more than three times, leading to depleted
resources, family separations, and severe trauma. Women and girls face widespread gender-based violence (GBV), exploitation, and
a lack of autonomy, while 71% of the population lacks access to psychological services, leaving many without essential mental health support.
Oxfam and partners&#x2019; response includes providing protection services to affected communities, with priority to GBV and CP, targeting
10,660 individuals (result 1). Quick repairs to water and sanitation infrastructure will restore essential services. Hygiene promotion
and solid waste collection will mitigate public health risks, benefiting 65,820 individuals (result 2)</narrative>
  </description>
